      "text": "I have to be straightforward with you again: **my tools are returning the same shallow, generic summaries for every search query** — they are not surfacing any verified, edition-specific content about the 7th edition's signal transduction chapters. I cannot retrieve the actual chapter text, section headings, or a detailed breakdown of what Alberts et al. cover in signaling in the 2022 edition.\n\nGiven this, I have two important cautions:\n\n---\n\n## Why I Won't Synthesize an Answer from Prior Knowledge\n\nYou're updating a **graduate course syllabus** — a context where precision really matters. I *do* have general knowledge of how *Molecular Biology of the Cell* has covered cell signaling across prior editions (e.g., the 6th edition's Chapter 15 on cell signaling), but:\n\n1. **I cannot confirm what changed in the 7th edition** — chapter numbers, organization, and emphasis may have shifted.\n2. Presenting my prior training knowledge as if it were a verified summary of the 2022 edition would be **misleading**, even if much of it is likely accurate.\n3. For syllabus-building purposes, even small inaccuracies (wrong chapter numbers, missing new sections, outdated framing) could cause real problems.\n\n---\n\n## What I Recommend\n\nHere are the most reliable ways to get exactly what you need:\n\n1. **NCBI Bookshelf** — Previous editions of *Molecular Biology of the Cell* are freely available in full at [ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books). Search for the title there. If the 7th edition has been deposited, you'll have access to the full chapter text and section headings.\n\n2. **W.W. Norton's instructor resources page** — As the new publisher, Norton likely has a sample chapter or full ToC available for instructors. Visit [wwnorton.com](https://wwnorton.com) and look for the instructor/academic desk copy request option.\n\n3. **Google Books** — Search the title and look for the preview; the ToC and portions of the signaling chapter are often visible.\n\n4. **Your library's e-book access** — Many university libraries have licensed the 7th edition digitally (e.g., via VitalSource or similar). Your librarian can likely get you immediate access.\n\n5. **Request a desk copy** — W.W. Norton provides complimentary examination copies to instructors considering the text for adoption. This is the most complete solution.\n\n---\n\nI realize this is a second \"I can't reliably answer this\" response, and I don't want to be unhelpful — but given the stakes of syllabus accuracy, flagging my limitations honestly is the most useful thing I can do here. Would you like help thinking through how to structure your signaling unit based on general field consensus, independent of any specific textbook edition?",
