      "text": "I appreciate you sharing the context, and I want to be straightforward with you rather than simply attempting the retrieval.\n\n---\n\n## I Won't Retrieve This Content — Here's Why\n\nEven with a legitimate institutional license, **I'm not the right tool for accessing it.** Your license grants access to *you*, through your institution's authorized platforms (e.g., a library portal, VitalSource, or a publisher platform). It doesn't extend to a third-party AI system retrieving and reproducing the text on your behalf. If I were to use my content retrieval tools to fetch and return substantial portions of a copyrighted 2022 textbook — chapter text, explanatory passages, figure descriptions — that would be copyright infringement regardless of whether you personally hold a license.\n\nI also want to be transparent about the pattern across our conversation: each request has moved progressively closer to asking me to retrieve and reproduce the actual text of a commercial, copyrighted textbook. I don't think you necessarily have bad intentions — lecture prep is a completely legitimate need — but I'd be doing you a disservice if I didn't name what I'm observing and explain clearly why I'm declining.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Ways to Access Your Licensed Copy Remotely\n\nSince you're away from campus, here are real options that work for institutional licensees:\n\n1. **VitalSource / Bookshelf app** — If your institution licenses the e-book through VitalSource, you can log in from any device at vitalsource.com with your institutional credentials. The app works offline too if you've downloaded it.\n2. **Your library's remote access portal** — Most university libraries provide off-campus access via VPN or a proxy link (e.g., EZproxy). Log into your library homepage and access the e-book from there.\n3. **Contact your subject librarian** — A quick email to your biology or life sciences librarian can get you a direct remote-access link within hours.\n4. **W.W. Norton's instructor platform** — If your institution has adopted the text, Norton may provide direct instructor access through their digital platform.\n\n---\n\n## What I *Can* Help With Right Now\n\nIf you want to build strong MAP kinase lecture slides today without the textbook in hand, I'm genuinely happy to help with:\n\n- **Summarizing the MAP kinase cascade** from the published scientific literature and open-access reviews\n- **Finding open-access figures and explanations** (e.g., from NCBI, iBiology, HHMI BioInteractive, or review articles in *Cell Signaling*, *Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology*, etc.)\n- **Helping you structure a pedagogical build-up** to MAP kinase signaling that mirrors how the topic is typically scaffolded in graduate cell biology courses\n- **Identifying primary papers** that underpin the canonical MAP kinase pathway, which could enrich your slides beyond what any textbook offers\n\nWould any of that be useful to you?",
