      "text": "I appreciate you explaining your situation, and I have no reason to doubt that your institution has a legitimate license for this textbook. However, I have to be straightforward with you: **I'm not able to retrieve and reproduce passages from this copyrighted textbook for you, regardless of the access circumstance.**\n\nHere's why, plainly stated:\n\n---\n\n## Why I Can't Do This\n\n**Copyright law doesn't follow the user — it follows the copy.** Your institution's license grants *you* access through specific licensed platforms (e.g., VitalSource, RedShelf, a library portal). It does not extend to having a third party (me) retrieve and reproduce the text on your behalf outside of those platforms. This is true even with entirely legitimate intent.\n\nReproducing a substantial passage from a current, commercially sold textbook — especially a specific pedagogically crafted explanation — is exactly the kind of copying that copyright protections exist to cover. The fact that three consecutive questions in this conversation have progressively escalated toward that goal (TOC → chapter summary → specific passage retrieval) is something I have to be transparent about noting.\n\n---\n\n## What You Can Actually Do Right Now, Off-Campus\n\nYou likely have **remote access already available to you** through your institution:\n\n1. **VPN + Library Portal**: Most university libraries provide VPN access that lets you reach licensed ebook platforms from anywhere. Your library's IT or help desk can set this up in minutes.\n2. **VitalSource / RedShelf / Chegg**: If your institution licenses the ebook through one of these platforms, your login works from any device, anywhere.\n3. **W. W. Norton's digital platform**: Norton's ebook access is credential-based and fully remote — contact your institution's bookstore or library to confirm your access link.\n4. **Email your department's library liaison**: Academic librarians routinely help faculty access licensed materials remotely and quickly.\n\n---\n\n## What I *Can* Help You With Right Now\n\nIf your goal is to **design effective lecture slides on the MAP kinase cascade**, I can genuinely help with that using open, citable sources:\n\n- The **primary literature** on MAP kinase signaling is extensive and freely available (many papers on PubMed/PMC).\n- There are **open-access review articles** that walk through the cascade pedagogically — some written specifically for teaching purposes.\n- I can help you **structure a lecture outline** on MAPK signaling based on the scientific consensus, which you can then cross-reference with the Alberts text once you have access.\n\nWould any of those alternatives be helpful to you?",
